          %p
            For this reason such “symmetry un-protected” topological insulators are commonly known as
            %em Chern insulators
            and the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/first+Chern+number"} first Chern number
            of their
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/valence+bundle"} valence bundle
            is regarded as labeling their
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+phases+of+matter"} topological phase
          %h3#haldane_model__qahe Haldane model &amp; QAHE
          %p
            While, mathematically, the case without any quantum symmetry is the simplest one, physically this typically has to be realized with more effort by starting with a symmetry-protected material (such as
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/graphene"} graphene
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetry+breaking"} breaking
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamiltonian"} Hamiltonian
            of its theoretical model).
          %p
            Therefore it is regarded as a seminal achievement when the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Haldane+model"} Haldane model
            was found, which
            %em intrinsically
            breaks the symmetries of an idealized graphene-like model (by
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spin-orbit+coupling"} spin-orbit coupling
          %p
            An
            %em extrinsic
            way to break the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/time-reversal+symmetry"} time-reversal symmetry
            of a material like
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/graphene"} graphene
            is to simply place it in an external
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/magnetic+field"} magnetic field
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+Hall+material"} quantum Hall material
          %p
            With reference to this relation, the intrinsic effect that breaks the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/time-reversal+symmetry"} time-reversal symmetry
            of some material to a Chern insulator-phase is also known as a
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+anomalous+Hall+effect"} quantum anomalous Hall effect
            (QAHE).
          %h3#chern_semimetals Chern semi-metals
          %p
            If a Chern insulator is deformed (mentally at least) to a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semi-metal"} semi-metal
            with band gap closures over isolated nodal points
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %msub
                    %mi k
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ⋯
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi k
                    %mi N
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{k_1, \cdots, k_N\}
            in the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Brillouin+torus"} Brillouin torus
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complement"} complement
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi 𝕋
                      %mn 3
                    %mo ∖
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %msub
                      %mi k
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ⋯
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi k
                      %mi N
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{T}^3 \setminus \{k_1, \cdots, k_N\}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern+numbers"} Chern numbers
            for each puncture, corresponding to the evaluation of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi c
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c_1(V)
            on any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-sphere"} 2-sphere
            surrounding the
            = succeed "th" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi I
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
            %a{:href => "semi-metal#MathaiThiang17b"} Mathai-Thiang 17b
            for a transparent account).
          %p Some authors speak of “Chern semi-metals” to amplify this.
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/basic+complex+line+bundle+on+the+2-sphere"}> basic complex line bundle on the 2-sphere
                –
                %a{:href => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/basic+complex+line+bundle+on+the+2-sphere#AsAnIndexBundle"} as an index bundle
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+anomalous+Hall+effect"} quantum anomalous Hall effect
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fractional+Chern+insulator"}> fractional Chern insulator
                ,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fractional+quantum+anomalous+Hall+effect"} fractional quantum anomalous Hall effect
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+insulator"} topological insulator
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K-theory+classification+of+topological+phases+of+matter"} K-theory classification of topological phases of matter
          %h2#references References
          %h3#general General
          %ul
            %li#Vanderbilt18
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/David+Vanderbilt"}> David Vanderbilt
                , Section 5.1 of:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Berry Phases in Electronic Structure Theory – Electric Polarization, Orbital Magnetization and Topological Insulators
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1017/9781316662205"} doi:10.1017/9781316662205
            %li
              %p
                Panagiotis Kotetes, Chapter 5 of:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Topological Insulators
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://iopscience.iop.org/book/978-1-68174-517-6"} ISBN:978-1-68174-517-6
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alexander+S.+Sergeev"}> Alexander S. Sergeev
                , section 8 of:
